Add zero-dependency security scanner, git hooks, CI, and docs

Repo is going public and is developed on a corporate machine, so the controls
target accidental disclosure rather than a targeted attacker.

security/scan.py  stdlib-only scanner: credentials, corporate identifiers,
                  internal URLs, local-path and tenant leakage. Scans added
                  lines only; redacts findings before printing, since CI logs
                  on a public repo are themselves public.
.githooks/        pre-commit (identity guard + staged scan), commit-msg,
                  pre-push (pushed range + full-history identity audit).
                  Version-controlled via core.hooksPath; .git/hooks neither
                  clones nor gets reviewed.
.github/workflows CI gates on the scanner, then a 2-OS x 2-python guard
                  matrix. A dedicated job proves the hooks actually block a
                  synthetic secret, so the control cannot silently rot.

Two encoding bugs found by dogfooding the scanner on its own commit:

  read   subprocess(text=True) decodes with the LOCALE codec (cp1252 on
         Windows). A non-ASCII byte in a diff raised UnicodeDecodeError inside
         subprocess's reader thread; the exception was swallowed and .stdout
         returned None. Had it returned an empty string the scanner would have
         reported CLEAN on content it never read - a silent false negative,
         the one failure mode that is unrecoverable for this tool.
  write  same root cause, opposite direction: printing a redacted line with a
         non-ASCII character killed the run AFTER a finding, losing the report
         exactly when it mattered.

Both now force UTF-8 and fail loudly rather than silently.

The CI OS matrix is load-bearing: the branch-cut test pins a floating-point
sign roughly 8 ULPs from flipping, which would silently invert every constant
derived from ln(-1).

45 new tests. All fixtures synthetic.

